      },
      "lineNbr": 6943,
      "author": {
        "id": 1054468
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-07T12:03:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For LSM hooks it\u0027s generally better to include more information. This allows individual LSMs to make granular decisions without having to continually touch/modify other parts of the kernel to pass additional information to the security hooks. With that in mind, why not use \"struct perf_event\" instead of \"perf_event-\u003eattr\" since that will also include perf_event_attr ?",
      "parentUuid": "4eda700f_1e7f30ad",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6943,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 6943,
        "endChar": 58
      },
      "revId": "b182040af765fc6cfded72e9732ce8926770a6fb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f5e7d5d4_6066525b",
        "filename": "security/selinux/hooks.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 6943,
      "author": {
        "id": 1054468
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-07T12:03:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this strictly necessary? It seems like this information might be available from a combination of perf_event_attr-\u003etype and perf_event_attr-\u003econfig. Alternatively, I wonder if we could/should just include that information in perf_event_attr.",
      "range": {
