    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "09f6a4e9_e4243e72",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/classmap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1054468
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-07T12:03:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How do we prevent already open perf_events from being passed (or leaked) to other processes by FD. What security label does the FD have? We prevent this for bpf FDs by tracking a sid along with the bpf object. Do we need something similar for perf_event FDs?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "b182040af765fc6cfded72e9732ce8926770a6fb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9d15b39b_47fe315f",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/classmap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124848
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-15T13:06:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Currently such a facility is not implemented. The default behavior should be DENY. However, I am not sure. What would happen, if we sent a perf fd over binder with the current patch?",
      "parentUuid": "09f6a4e9_e4243e72",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "b182040af765fc6cfded72e9732ce8926770a6fb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf68d344_14d58b63",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/classmap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124848
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-02T17:14:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Jeff, could you clarify if FD passing is always denied unless explicty allowed by SeLinux? I am curious what happens if we don\u0027t implement this functionality for perf.",
      "parentUuid": "9d15b39b_47fe315f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "b182040af765fc6cfded72e9732ce8926770a6fb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "02d482c4_30bb798d",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/classmap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1054468
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-02T18:33:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is default deny, but since FD passing is so common on Android that\u0027s not a useful protection i.e. processes are very commonly allowed to use FDs from another process, which is why we need to also protect the object that is represented by that FD.\n\nAs an example, all apps are allowed to handle FDs from system_server, but we wouldn\u0027t want just any object represented by FD to be usable by an app, otherwise we risk object leakage. Apps are specifically only allowed to handle pipes and tcp_sockets passed by system_server. It would be nice to make the same assertions about perf_event objects.\nhttps://android.googlesource.com/platform/system/sepolicy/+/refs/heads/master/public/app.te#53",
      "parentUuid": "bf68d344_14d58b63",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "b182040af765fc6cfded72e9732ce8926770a6fb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "318634d8_3808ac90",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/classmap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1040443
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-02T21:52:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm not sure if I am interpreting this conversation right but I think we actually do want the ability to restrict only open() but allow pass the FD around to another process that doesn\u0027t have perf_event_open capabilities.\n\nThe problem is the following: things are complicated (as usual).\nOnly few privileged processes on Android will be allowed to perf_event_open. but unfortunately those processes won\u0027t be able to unwind frames correctly unless they runs in the same selinux sandbox of the profiled process (this is because ART symbols cannot be accessed by other sandboxes).\nBut we don\u0027t want apps to generally perf_event_open(). \nSo what will happen in practice is that we\u0027ll likely have to fork() a daemon from the app sandbox for the sake of unwinding, do perf_event_open from another privileged process and then pass the FD to the unwinding app.",
      "parentUuid": "02d482c4_30bb798d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 246,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "b182040af765fc6cfded72e9732ce8926770a6fb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cf75bf5e_0d06d172",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/classmap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 246,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124848
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-03T15:05:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Jeff,\nI will look into allocating a new SID for perf_event objects like BPF does. That should take care of what you are asking.\n\nPrimiano,\nI think I am not following. Can you pen down the design you have in mind? It is good if we keep it simple. Dumping stack pointers can also be done from the process that does the open. Then these pointers/stack-frames can be passed to the unwinding app. That keeps it simple without having to pass around FDs.",
      "parentUuid": "318634d8_3808ac90",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 246,
        "startChar": 4,
